    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Has this forgetfulness been a problem your entire life, or is this a recent change that has developed over the last few years?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, it's definitely not new. It feels like it's been my whole life, really. Even as a boy in grade school, I was always that kid who just couldn’t stay in his seat. I'd blurt out answers all the time and I was constantly getting notes sent home to my parents for talking too much. \n\nMy mother used to keep all those notes in this old wooden cigar box on top of the refrigerator. It was an El Producto box—George Burns used to smoke those, you know. Man, I used to love watching George and Gracie on the television. We had this big, heavy Zenith set in the living room that took about five minutes just to warm up the tubes before you even got a picture. You had to smack the side of it sometimes to stop the screen from rolling. My dad finally fixed it with a piece of folded-up cardboard shoved under the dial. He was a real tinkerer, my dad. Could fix an engine with his eyes closed, but he wouldn't pay a dime for a repairman if he could rig it up himself. \n\nBut anyway, what was I saying? Oh, right. No, it's not a recent change at all. I've pretty much always been like this.",
