      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "When you are working on those repair projects, do you find that you often overlook small details or make careless mistakes in your work?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, no, not really. I don't think I make careless mistakes when I'm fixing things. I mean, I might get distracted and start working on something else entirely, but the work I actually do is pretty solid. \n\nTake that coffee machine down at the center, for example. I didn't get it put all the way back together last time because I realized the heating element was completely shot. It's one of those old aluminum coils, you know? They don't make them like that anymore. Now everything is just cheap plastic and circuit boards that fry if you look at them wrong. My grandson bought this fancy espresso maker—cost him an arm and a leg, probably more than my first car did. And what happens? Three months in, a little green light starts blinking and the whole thing just shuts down. Can't even make a regular cup of joe. He had to box the whole thing up and mail it to some warehouse in New Jersey just to get it looked at. In the old days, you could just pop the back off, find the loose wire, and solder it yourself. I've got a soldering iron in my garage that I've had since 1975. Still works perfectly. \n\nBut no, I wouldn't say I make careless mistakes. I just... well, I might not always finish what I started because I find something else that needs fixing first.",
