      "doctor_question": "When you are in social situations or talking with others, do you often find that you interrupt people, finish their sentences, or have a hard time waiting for your turn to speak?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S018",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah, I suppose I do that a fair bit. Like down at the senior center, if I hear folks at the next table discussing a repair project or arguing over a hand of pinochle, I'll just jump right in and give them my two cents before anyone even asks me. I can usually tell right after I do it that I just barged in where I wasn't invited, but it just slips out. \n\nSpeaking of pinochle, they use these awful plastic-coated cards down there now. They stick together when the humidity gets high. My uncle Arthur, he taught me how to play cards, and he swore by those old paper Bicycle decks. He used to say you could tell a lot about a man by how he shuffled a deck of cards. Uncle Arthur was a character, let me tell you. He worked on the railroad for forty years, lost half his left index finger coupling cars in the rail yard in Chicago. Never slowed him down, though. He could still deal a hand of poker faster than anyone I ever met. He had this little trick where he'd flip the cards... \n\nBut yeah, what were we talking about? Oh, right, interrupting. Yeah, I definitely have a bad habit of jumping into other people's business before they ask for my opinion.",
