    {
      "turn": 7,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back at your life, have you consistently struggled with both the focus issues and the need to be constantly moving, or has one of those been a much bigger problem for you than the other?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, it's really always been the moving around and the talking that got me in the most trouble. Even as a boy in grade school, I was the kid who just couldn’t stay in his seat. I always blurted out answers before the teacher even called on anyone, and I got so many notes sent home for talking too much. \n\nI remember my fourth-grade teacher, Mrs. Gable, she actually started pinning those notes right to my jacket collar with a safety pin so I couldn't \"accidentally\" lose them on the walk home. We had to walk a good two miles back then, right down Elm Street. Elm Street used to have these massive, beautiful elm trees, right up until the Dutch elm disease wiped them all out in the sixties. It was a real shame, you know? The whole neighborhood looked completely different after the city came and chopped them down. Just bare. \n\nMy dad actually saved some of the wood from the one they took down in our front yard. He tried to build a coffee table out of it in the garage. But elm is a really tough wood to work with. The grain is all interlocked, so it splits if you don't know exactly what you're doing with the saw. He spent three weekends swearing at that wood before he ended up just tossing the whole thing in the wood chipper. We had this big old gas-powered chipper back then, loud as a jet engine. You'd have to wear earplugs just to stand in the driveway... \n\nBut, uh, anyway. What was I saying? Oh, right. Yeah, the moving around and the blurting things out. That's always been the biggest issue for me, hands down, my whole life.",
